{
  "schema_version": "1.0",
  "encounter_id": "00748",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0001",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 1.94,
      "end_seconds": 14.28,
      "duration_seconds": 12.34,
      "text": "Doctor, I'm worried. I've been waking at night in a sweat, shaking, and my meter says my sugar's low. It's happened three times this month."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0002",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 17.38,
      "end_seconds": 25.07,
      "duration_seconds": 7.69,
      "text": "Thank you for telling me, that's important. What does the meter say, when it happens?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0003",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 28.22,
      "end_seconds": 37.57,
      "duration_seconds": 9.35,
      "text": "Two point eight, once. Three point one another time. I ate some biscuits and it came up."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0004",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 40.63,
      "end_seconds": 46.36,
      "duration_seconds": 5.73,
      "text": "Do you lose consciousness, or have a fit, when it happens?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0005",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 48.72,
      "end_seconds": 57.31,
      "duration_seconds": 8.59,
      "text": "No, no, I don't pass out, and I've never had a fit. I wake up myself."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0006",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 59.5,
      "end_seconds": 63.23,
      "duration_seconds": 3.73,
      "text": "Let's go through your treatment."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0007",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 65.24,
      "end_seconds": 74.55,
      "duration_seconds": 9.31,
      "text": "Insulin glargine, 20 units at night. Metformin, 1000 mg twice a day. And gliclazide, 80 mg twice a day."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0008",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 77.59,
      "end_seconds": 83.36,
      "duration_seconds": 5.77,
      "text": "And do you take the gliclazide even when you haven't eaten?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0009",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 85.96,
      "end_seconds": 96.37,
      "duration_seconds": 10.41,
      "text": "I try to, but if I skip dinner, I sometimes skip it as well. I'm not always sure what I've done."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0010",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 99.85,
      "end_seconds": 111.21,
      "duration_seconds": 11.36,
      "text": "Thank you for being honest. I'll note that the gliclazide is meant to be twice a day, but you're not always sure. Any allergies?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0011",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 113.78,
      "end_seconds": 115.65,
      "duration_seconds": 1.87,
      "text": "No, none."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0012",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 118.21,
      "end_seconds": 119.64,
      "duration_seconds": 1.43,
      "text": "Medical history?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0013",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 122.93,
      "end_seconds": 127.79,
      "duration_seconds": 4.86,
      "text": "Type 2 diabetes, fifteen years. And high blood pressure."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0014",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 129.68,
      "end_seconds": 131.47,
      "duration_seconds": 1.79,
      "text": "Any operations?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0015",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 134.63,
      "end_seconds": 138.91,
      "duration_seconds": 4.28,
      "text": "Cataracts, both eyes, three years ago."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0016",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 142.06,
      "end_seconds": 148.68,
      "duration_seconds": 6.62,
      "text": "What time do you have your evening meal, and how much? ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0017",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 151.1,
      "end_seconds": 159.95,
      "duration_seconds": 8.85,
      "text": "It varies. Sometimes seven, sometimes nine. Lighter, lately, since I've been trying to lose weight. ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0018",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 162.6,
      "end_seconds": 179.81,
      "duration_seconds": 17.21,
      "text": "That's the clue. Gliclazide pushes your body to make insulin whether or not you've eaten, and on top of the long-acting insulin, a lighter dinner can send your sugar too low at night. ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0019",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 183.02,
      "end_seconds": 186.35,
      "duration_seconds": 3.33,
      "text": "So it's the tablets? ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0020",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 188.85,
      "end_seconds": 199.63,
      "duration_seconds": 10.78,
      "text": "It's the combination. I'd say these night-time lows are most likely due to the gliclazide and insulin together with smaller meals."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0021",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 203.05,
      "end_seconds": 206.12,
      "duration_seconds": 3.07,
      "text": "Do I stop something? ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0022",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 208.84,
      "end_seconds": 222.69,
      "duration_seconds": 13.85,
      "text": "I'd like you to cut the gliclazide to 40 mg twice a day and keep the insulin the same for now. Eat a small snack with carbohydrate at bedtime."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0023",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 224.77,
      "end_seconds": 228.67,
      "duration_seconds": 3.9,
      "text": "Right. Will it get high? ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0024",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 232.1,
      "end_seconds": 245.05,
      "duration_seconds": 12.95,
      "text": "Your sugars may run a little higher, which is safer than lows. I'd like to arrange an HbA1c blood test, to see the longer-term picture."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0025",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 248.43,
      "end_seconds": 252.71,
      "duration_seconds": 4.28,
      "text": "Fair. What if it happens again? ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0026",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 256.16,
      "end_seconds": 268.92,
      "duration_seconds": 12.76,
      "text": "Treat with fast sugar, fifteen to twenty grams, a glass of juice, retest in fifteen minutes, then eat a snack. And ring us with your readings."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0027",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 271.77,
      "end_seconds": 276.53,
      "duration_seconds": 4.76,
      "text": "I'll write it on the fridge. ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0028",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 279.08,
      "end_seconds": 289.53,
      "duration_seconds": 10.45,
      "text": "Please do. If you ever can't wake properly or can't treat it yourself, your family should call 999."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0029",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 292.81,
      "end_seconds": 299.13,
      "duration_seconds": 6.32,
      "text": "Right. Thank you, doctor. Should I carry something sweet in my pocket? ~"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0030",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 301.25,
      "end_seconds": 306.01,
      "duration_seconds": 4.76,
      "text": "Yes, glucose tablets or jelly babies, always. ~"
    }
  ]
}
